# Build artifacts. `go build ./...` inside a module drops the binary in the
# module directory; `make build` puts them all in bin/ instead.
agent/agent
controller/hack/capd-reboot-controller/capd-reboot-controller
**/*.test
**/bin/
# Separate from **/bin/ on purpose: a leading dot is not matched by that
# pattern, and 141 MB of downloaded tools reached main through exactly this
# gap.
**/.bin/
**/dist/
**/release/
# The root release/ is tracked: it holds release tooling, not build output.
# The rule above still catches a nested release/ a build drops somewhere, which
# is what it was written for. Re-including the directory is what makes this
# work -- git does not descend into an excluded directory, so a negation naming
# a file inside it would never match.
!/release/

# `qualify plan --output plan.json`, written at the checkout root. Anchored to
# the root, since --output defaults to stdout and a run has to ask for the file
# by name.
/plan.json

# Coverage output and local cluster credentials.
*.out
*.kubeconfig

# Go workspace dependencies may be vendored locally for offline remote tests,
# but the generated tree is not committed.
/vendor/

# Reference-image Packer output and build-only SSH keys / pulled binaries.
os-image/packer/output/
os-image/packer/output-fde/
os-image/packer/output-non-fde/
os-image/packer/artifacts/
os-image/packer/.artifacts.tmp.*/
os-image/packer/.artifacts.backup.*/
os-image/packer/.packer-inputs/
os-image/packer/.packer-inputs.tmp.*/
os-image/packer/.packer-output/
os-image/packer/.packer-output-fde/
os-image/packer/.packer-output-non-fde/
os-image/packer/iso-cache/
os-image/packer/build-logs/
os-image/packer/.ssh/

# qcow-imager.efi build. .infra-controller/ is the pinned upstream checkout the
# build runs in; it also holds mkosi's package cache, which is most of the disk.
os-image/efi/.infra-controller/
os-image/efi/output/

# Personal environment read by .envrc. It holds credentials, so the ignore has to
# sit wherever .envrc does. The rule was in controller/.gitignore and scoped to
# that directory.
.env.local

# Editor / OS
.idea/
.vscode/
*.code-workspace
*.swp
*.swo
*~
.DS_Store

# go.work and go.work.sum are tracked, not ignored. The usual advice to keep a
# Per-checkout tool caches and scratch output. controller/.gitignore already
# ignores its own .local/; make/golangci.mk puts the golangci-lint cache in this
# one so two checkouts cannot replay each other's issues.
.local/
# Python bytecode from running the hack/ scripts and their tests locally.
__pycache__/

# workspace local applies to a workspace that papers over module boundaries
# during development; this one is the build definition for every module that
# releases, and CI verifies it is current (`make work-check`).

# The etcd encryption key `nvkectl bootstrap site` writes, and the temporary
# file it is assembled in: into the working directory by default, so the rule
# lives at the root. Both patterns are needed; the first cannot match a
# leading-dot name.
*-etcd-encryption-key.json
.*-etcd-encryption-key.json.*
cli/e2e/bootstrap/.etcd-encryption-key.json

# The secret values the bootstrap E2E's Go test saves, in clear. See cli/.gitignore.
cli/e2e/bootstrap/.bootstrap-known-secrets.json

# nvkectl and the three image archives `make site-inputs` stages for the Forge
# site-controller lane. The lane points SITE_INPUTS here, inside the checkout,
# so the workspace rather than a $HOME cache owns them -- see the env block in
# qualify-e2e.yml. Hundreds of megabytes of docker save output.
.site-inputs/

# What the Forge lane's `go test` leaves in the e2e directory. qualify-e2e.report.json and
# the diagnostics tarball carry captured spec output, which on this lane is
# remote command output, so an accidental commit is a disclosure rather than
# noise.
controller/test/e2e/qualify-e2e.log
controller/test/e2e/qualify-e2e.junit.xml
controller/test/e2e/qualify-e2e.report.json
controller/test/e2e/nvkectl-diagnostics.tar.gz
controller/test/e2e/site-cluster-state.txt
controller/test/e2e/tenant-diagnostics.json
controller/test/e2e/site-bundle*

# Coverage profiles. make/coverage.mk points every module's `go test` here, and
# hack/coverage.sh reads the directory. Nothing in it is committed: a profile
# describes one run on one machine, and CI uploads it as an artifact instead.
/coverage/
